From: Loong <1031313955@qq.com>
Subject: [PATCH] xg-040g-md: let the recovery page put the factory image back

Going back to stock was the one direction the page could not go.  The ways
out were tcboot's own web interface -- which is gone the moment you migrate
away from it -- or a serial console with a TFTP server, which is exactly the
setup this page exists to avoid needing.

Two operations, and they are less alike than they look:

  - whole chip, for actually going back.  A partition map does not live on
    the flash: it comes from the device tree, which arrives with the
    bootloader and the kernel, so restoring the factory bytes at the factory
    offsets brings the factory map back with them.  That is why a U-Boot
    whose own map is just bl2 + ubi can do this at all -- "mtd write" on the
    raw device takes byte offsets and never asks what a partition is called.
    It has to be the raw device for the same reason: bl2 ends at 0x20000 and
    ubi begins there, so neither can reach offset 0 with a factory-sized
    write.  all_flash.bin is 235.625 MiB and its length is checked before
    anything is erased, since a short file here means erasing the board and
    then finding out;

  - one factory volume, which is the common case and the safe one.  This is
    NOT the same operation at a smaller size.  Stock keeps ri as an mtd
    partition at 0x5200000, which on this layout is 80 MiB inside the ubi
    partition; writing raw bytes there would tear through UBI's erase-counter
    and volume-id headers and take the volume table with it -- and it would
    not even work, because ethaddr_factory does "ubi read ... ri" and so
    reads the volume, never the offset.  Same contents, same length, the MAC
    still at +0x3e, different container.  So these go back as UBI volumes,
    which touches nothing else.

Restoring ri is how a board gets its factory MAC back after "rebuild UBI"
wiped it; without it the only copy left is the label on the case.

UPLOAD_MAX becomes a computed ceiling.  The upload lands at $loadaddr and is
flashed from where it landed, so what fits is whatever lies between there and
U-Boot itself -- and U-Boot relocated to the top of DRAM, so the top of DRAM
is not the answer.  gd->start_addr_sp is the bottom edge of its code, malloc
arena, FDT and stack, and the stack grows down from there, which is what the
margin covers.  A fixed 96 MiB limit would have refused a 235.6 MiB factory
image outright; simply raising the constant would let a larger one run into
U-Boot mid-transfer.

The stock paths never share a session with the UBI-side writes -- the form
refuses to submit them together, and httpd_flash() returns after either
rather than falling through into the bl2/fip/fit sequence.

